{"video_id": "ud1ESmogrFY", "source_file": "America Has No Plan B.mp4", "source_sha256": "242114c80b20d2104a5588fe86da697e430323037f58957577e820bb4f06dc94", "local_dur": 86.85, "model": "base", "engine": "openai-whisper", "status": "ASR_UNCERTIFIED", "device": "cpu", "elapsed_s": 9.6, "language": "en", "text": " The single biggest threat to America isn't the national debt. It's not the straight of hormones, and it's not even the AI bubble itself. It's energy. But not energy in the way you're probably thinking. It's not blackouts or your monthly utility bill. It's that America has pushed all its chips to the center of the table and bet the entire economy on one wager that AI arrives on schedule. But if something's going to pop the AI bubble, it's energy. And here's the thing about all those bubble takes. Every single one of them is arguing about the wrong question. They're arguing about whether AI is here to stay, whether it's truly going to revolutionize every industry, and whether a chatbot that can't count the letters in strawberry is maybe about to take your job. But that was never the right question. The real bet isn't whether AI works. It's actually about whether we can even physically deliver it on time. Whether the AI future can arrive fast enough to justify the insane prices we're paying for it today. Because right now, money is disappearing into AI faster than Thanos can snap his fingers. It's the fastest and largest build out in history, yet nobody's actually making any money on it, which is fine for now. Investors will happily fund losses as long as they think a payoff is on its way. But eventually, the investors, the companies, and the lenders funding all of this need that payoff to actually show up. Eventually, the capital has to come home with a return. That's how the AI bubble pops. It's not when it fails, but when it's late. When the capital starts to get sick of waiting around and decides to go somewhere else.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "st